    slug: 'rhode-island-unauthorized-life-insurance-shared-producer-login-scheme',
    description:
      'A Rhode Island Department of Business Regulation (DBR) investigation, resolved by a March 27, 2025 consent agreement, laid out how an insurance producer\'s credentials were used to open life insurance policies on real people who never applied for them. After a November 30, 2023 consumer complaint alleged that producers Terrell Gray and Michael Hutchinson had opened a life insurance policy without the policyholder\'s permission, DBR\'s Insurance Division found that the underlying carrier had already terminated both men for cause over unauthorized sales, and that a Cumberland, Rhode Island consumer and two Connecticut consumers it contacted separately all said the same thing: they had never applied for the policy issued in their name and did not know the producer who supposedly wrote it. Gray admitted he had let Hutchinson — his manager at the time — log into Gray\'s own agent portal to help with a technical issue, not realizing Hutchinson was using that access to submit new life insurance applications under Gray\'s producer code; in total, Gray\'s code was used to submit 15 applications and issue 9 policies for Rhode Island and Connecticut consumers between June and July 2023, generating commissions Gray then paid out to Hutchinson even while Hutchinson\'s own producer license was inactive for a 115-day stretch that year. DBR had already suspended Hutchinson\'s Rhode Island license in February 2024 and revoked it that March after he failed to appear at his own hearing on the underlying fraud complaints; Gray, whom investigators found had cooperated once he learned what happened but had shared login credentials in violation of state data-security rules, agreed to a $375 fine and acknowledged the Division\'s findings without contesting them. Because the agent portal Hutchinson accessed displayed full policyholder names and policy numbers, the episode also exposed how easily one producer\'s login can become a backdoor into other consumers\' personal information once it is shared, even informally, between colleagues. Anyone who receives an unfamiliar insurance bill, a welcome packet for a policy they never bought, or a call about a life insurance "withdrawal" they never requested should treat it as a sign an unauthorized policy may have been opened in their name, and can confirm an agent is actually licensed and currently in good standing through DBR\'s free producer lookup before assuming a policy is legitimate.',
