    description: `New York Attorney General Letitia James issued a consumer alert on February 17, 2026, warning residents about "pig butchering" scams — schemes in which fraudsters "fatten up" a victim by building trust over weeks or months before slaughtering their savings in a single fraudulent investment. The scams typically begin with an unsolicited connection on social media, a dating app, or a text message that appears to be a wrong number, with the scammer working to build a friendship or romance before steering the conversation onto an encrypted app like WhatsApp or WeChat. Once trust is established, the scammer introduces what looks like an exclusive trading or cryptocurrency investment opportunity, often walking the victim through a slick-looking app or website that shows fabricated returns to encourage larger and larger deposits — money that, once sent, cannot be recovered. "Heartless scammers are using increasingly sophisticated means to prey on communities looking for connection and opportunity," James said, adding that "these fraudsters go to great lengths to gain trust and convince people to send them their savings, sometimes ruining their victims' finances." James's office noted victims have reported losses ranging from tens of thousands of dollars to over a million dollars apiece. Red flags her office listed include being told to keep the relationship or investment secret, being coached on what to say if a bank or brokerage asks questions about a wire, being pressured to move money quickly, being asked to hand cash to a courier or use a bitcoin ATM, and a romantic interest who always has an excuse for why they can't meet in person. James urged New Yorkers to never wire money, send cryptocurrency, or hand cash to someone they have never met, to independently research anyone who solicits an investment, to consult a trusted financial or legal professional before investing, and to save all messages as evidence if they suspect they're being targeted. Anyone affected is urged to file a complaint with the Attorney General's office at ag.ny.gov or call 1-800-771-7755.`,
