    description: `North Carolina Attorney General Jeff Jackson issued a consumer alert on May 5, 2026, warning licensed professionals across the state — including nurses, contractors, real estate agents, and other license holders — about scam calls impersonating their state licensing board. The callers often arrive already armed with publicly available details, such as the target's real name, license number, or professional identification number, which they use to sound credible from the first moment of the call. Once they have the target's attention, the caller falsely claims the licensee is under criminal investigation or that their license is at risk of suspension, using that manufactured fear to pressure the person into handing over personal information or making an immediate payment to resolve the "investigation." Jackson's office stressed that this playbook is not how any real licensing board operates: "These scammers do their homework. They may have your license number and other real details to make the call sound believable. Then they say you're under investigation to scare you into paying or sharing personal information. No licensing board works this way. If you get a call like this, hang up and call the board using the number on its official website." The alert cautioned licensees not to rely on caller ID to verify who is calling, since that information can be spoofed, and to always look up a licensing agency's number independently — through its official website — rather than calling back a number the caller provides. North Carolinians who receive one of these calls are urged to hang up, verify directly with their board, and report the call to the North Carolina Department of Justice's Consumer Protection Division at ncdoj.gov or by calling 1-877-5-NO-SCAM.`,
