    description: `New York Attorney General Letitia James issued a consumer alert on August 7, 2026 warning of a "gold bar scam" that has drained more than $100 million from victims statewide, with the NYPD alone investigating over 100 cases in the past two years. The scheme starts with a fake pop-up warning on a victim's computer, styled to look like a legitimate antivirus or bank security alert, claiming their device or financial accounts have been compromised. Calling the number in the pop-up connects the victim to a scammer posing as tech support, who transfers them to a second scammer posing as a bank fraud investigator or law-enforcement official. That fake investigator convinces the victim — usually an older adult — that their accounts have already been infiltrated and that the only way to protect their life savings is to withdraw the money, convert it into gold bars or coins, and hand the gold to a courier who will supposedly hold it safely until the "investigation" concludes; victims are coached to lie to their own bank tellers about why they're withdrawing large sums and to keep the scheme secret from family members who might intervene. Once the courier collects the gold in person, it is gone for good. "Targeting older adults with seemingly legitimate claims that their life savings are in immediate danger is flat-out cruel," James said in announcing the alert. Her office advises anyone who sees such a pop-up to never call the number it displays, never grant a stranger remote access to a computer, and never withdraw money or buy gold on a caller's instruction — instead hang up and verify any claimed account problem by calling the bank directly using the number on a genuine statement or card. Suspected gold bar scams can be reported to the Attorney General's office online or at 1-800-771-7755.`,
