    slug: 'new-york-colombia-earthquake-charity-scam',
    description: `New York Attorney General Letitia James issued a consumer alert on August 13, 2026 warning New Yorkers to be cautious before donating to relief efforts following a devastating earthquake in Colombia, cautioning that "scammers are especially active following national disasters" and urging residents to "be careful of sham charities and make sure they are giving to trustworthy organizations." James's office identified several tactics bad actors were already using to exploit the tragedy, including fraudulent email solicitations requesting personal or credit card information, fake fundraising campaigns spun up on social media and crowdfunding platforms, newly formed organizations with no real infrastructure to deliver aid, unsolicited text messages soliciting donations, and outfits that copy a legitimate charity's name or logo to look authentic. The alert advised donors to check a charity's registration and financial history with the Attorney General's Charities Bureau before giving, ask specifically how a donation will be used, avoid handing over unnecessary personal information, and pay by check or credit card rather than cash, gift cards, or wire transfers, which offer no way to recover the money once sent. Because individual crowdfunding organizers are not registered charities and donations to them are not tax-deductible or bound by charity-oversight rules, James's office also recommended researching a campaign's organizer directly before contributing and consulting established, well-known relief organizations such as the American Red Cross when in doubt. New Yorkers who suspect a fundraiser is fraudulent can file a complaint with the Charities Bureau or email complaints@ag.ny.gov.`,
