    name: 'Carolina Hurricanes Stanley Cup Final Resale Ticket Scam',
    slug: 'north-carolina-stanley-cup-final-ticket-scam',
    description: `As the Carolina Hurricanes' run to the Stanley Cup Final sent resale ticket prices skyrocketing — with some single seats listed as high as $17,000 and even standing-room-only spots going for as much as $600 — North Carolina Attorney General Jeff Jackson issued a consumer alert on June 5, 2026 warning fans that the same excitement driving up demand was creating ideal conditions for scammers to sell tickets that would never actually get a buyer into the arena. Jackson urged fans to "make sure you know who you're buying tickets from and think twice before you buy a resold ticket," and his office laid out specific precautions: buy only through reputable, verified platforms and check that a site's web address begins with "https" and shows a lock icon before entering payment information; be skeptical of sellers who create false urgency with a sudden family emergency or other hardship story to pressure a fast purchase; pay by credit card rather than debit card or a peer-to-peer payment app, since credit cards carry stronger fraud-dispute protections; and confirm a seller's refund policy is clearly stated before paying, since a legitimate reseller will readily answer questions about returns. The alert came alongside separate scrutiny of Ticketmaster itself, after Hurricanes season-ticket holders with presale access codes reported being pushed to the back of the platform's virtual queue during the Stanley Cup Final on-sale. Fans who believe they were sold a fake or non-existent ticket can file a complaint with the North Carolina Department of Justice online or by calling 1-877-5-NO-SCAM.`,
