    description: `Oscar Peters, a 65-year-old British national, met his victim, a Portland-area woman, through the online dating website Millionaire Match Maker, where he presented himself as a billionaire living in Denmark who was seeking a serious, long-term relationship. Over roughly two years he maintained daily contact through romantic emails and phone calls, promised marriage, and told her he planned to move to Portland to be with her — while repeatedly asking her for money, claiming his soon-to-be ex-wife had frozen his assets or that he needed funds to complete business obligations, each time assuring her he would pay her back once his finances were unfrozen. In total, Peters stole approximately $1.9 million from the victim. A federal grand jury in the District of Oregon indicted Peters on seven counts of wire fraud in June 2019; he was arrested in the United Kingdom in April 2020 and fought extradition until October 2023, when he was returned to the U.S. to face the charges. He pleaded guilty to wire fraud, and on June 4, 2025 a U.S. District Court judge sentenced him to 37 months in federal prison and three years of supervised release, and ordered him to pay $1,892,439 in restitution. The case is a reminder that a "billionaire" who never appears on a video call, never meets in person, and repeatedly needs money transferred to unfreeze assets he claims to already have is describing a scam, not a temporary financial setback, no matter how long or how convincingly the online relationship has built trust.`,
    categorySlug: 'romance-scams',
    alertLevel: 'medium',
    sources: ["U.S. Attorney's Office, District of Oregon", 'KPTV', 'Portland Tribune'],
    sourceUrl: 'https://www.justice.gov/usao-or/pr/british-man-sentenced-federal-prison-stealing-nearly-19-million-romance-fraud-scheme',
